As discussed here, I ported a proc macro called `exec_spec!` into Verus from another project.
Basically, it is a compiler from `struct`/`enum`/`spec fn`'s to their verified, executable versions.
The following example shows some of the spec features it supports:
```rust
use vstd::prelude::*;
use vstd::exec_spec::*;
use exec_spec::exec_spec;
verus! {
exec_spec! {
struct MyPair(Seq<u32>, Seq<u32>);
spec fn pred_helper(a: Seq<u32>, b: Seq<u32>, i: usize) -> bool
recommends 0 <= i < a.len() == b.len()
decreases a.len() - i when i < usize::MAX
{
&&& a[i as int] > b[i as int]
&&& i + 1 < a.len() ==> pred_helper(a, b, (i + 1) as usize)
}
// Checks element-wise if p.0 > p.1
spec fn pred(p: MyPair) -> bool
recommends p.0.len() == p.1.len()
{
if p.0.len() == 0 {
true
} else {
pred_helper(p.0, p.1, 0)
}
}
}
fn test() {
let a = ExecMyPair(vec![1, 2], vec![0, 1]);
if exec_pred(&a) {
assert(a.0@[0] > a.1@[0]);
assert(a.0@[1] > a.1@[1]);
}
}
}
```
In this example, the `exec_spec!` macro generates three new items: `ExecMyPair`, `exec_pred_helper`, and `exec_pred`, with the following impls and specs:
- `ExecMyPair` implements `DeepView `
- `exec_pred_helper` has the following verified spec (simplified):
```rust
exec_pred_helper(a: &[u32], b: &[u32], i: usize) -> (res: bool)
requires 0 <= i < a.deep_view().len() == b.deep_view().len(), // converted from `recommends`
i < usize::MAX, // converted from the `when` clause
ensures res == pred_helper(a.deep_view(), b.deep_view(), i) // equivalent to pred_helper
decreases a.deep_view().len() - i
```
- Similar specs for `exec_pred`
Besides adding these new exec counterparts, `exec_spec!` keeps all items passed to it unmodified.
